+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer Resolution
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-json-pointer`
+**Status**: Draft
+**Input**: "Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901): export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+
+### User Story 1 - Resolve a pointer to a value (Priority: P1)
+A service author gives a JSON document and a JSON Pointer string and gets back the referenced value.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+1. Given any document, when the pointer is `""`, then the whole document is returned.
+2. Given `{"a":{"b":[10,20]}}`, when the pointer is `/a/b/1`, then `20` is returned.
+3. Given `{"a/b":1,"m~n":2}`, when the pointer is `/a~1b` or `/m~0n`, then `1` or `2` is returned (`~1` → `/`, `~0` → `~`; `~01` decodes to `~1`).
+4. Given `{"":1," ":2}`, when the pointer is `/` or `/ `, then `1` or `2` is returned (empty-string key).
+5. Given a value that is `null`, `false`, `0` or `""`, when a pointer refers to it, then that value is returned (it is a value of the document).
+
+### User Story 2 - Reject invalid or unresolvable pointers (Priority: P1)
+Callers get an error rather than a silent `undefined`.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+1. Given a non-empty pointer not starting with `/` (e.g. `a/b`), an error is thrown.
+2. Given a `~` not followed by `0` or `1` (e.g. `/a~`, `/a~2`), an error is thrown.
+3. Given a pointer naming a missing object member, an error is thrown.
+4. Given an array and a token that is not valid array index syntax (`-`, `01`, `+1`, `1.0`, `a`) an error is thrown; `-` refers to a nonexistent element so it never resolves.
+5. Given an index beyond the array length, an error is thrown.
+6. Given a pointer that descends into a string, number, boolean or `null`, an error is thrown.
+7. Given a non-string pointer, an error is thrown.
+
+### Edge Cases
+- Inherited properties (`/constructor`, `/__proto__`, `/toString`) are not members of the document and must not resolve.
+- Array "length" and other non-index properties must not resolve.
+- Array indexes are `0` or digits without leading zeros.
+- Pointers are used as-is (no URI fragment decoding; `#` forms are out of scope).
+
+## Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` MUST be exported from `src/index.ts`.
+- **FR-002**: The empty pointer MUST return the whole document.
+- **FR-003**: Pointers MUST follow the RFC 6901 syntax: zero or more `/`-prefixed reference tokens; `~0`/`~1` are the only escapes, decoded `~1` first then `~0` semantics per the RFC (i.e., single-pass so `~01` → `~1`).
+- **FR-004**: Object tokens MUST match own members by exact string; array tokens MUST be valid indexes within bounds.
+- **FR-005**: Any invalid pointer, or any pointer not resolving to a value, MUST throw an `Error`; a resolved falsy/`null` value MUST be returned normally.
+- **FR-006**: The function MUST NOT modify the document.
+
+## Success Criteria
+
+- **SC-001**: Every example in RFC 6901 section 5 resolves to the documented value.
+- **SC-002**: Every invalid or unresolvable case above throws, with zero silent `undefined` results.
+
+## Assumptions
+- The document is parsed JSON data (objects, arrays, primitives); the string form of the pointer only, not URI fragment form.
+- Later features (e.g. update operations) will reuse this behaviour; pointer parsing is kept separable.
